<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of the Moors and Romans</h1><p class="st-meta">477 CE – 578 CE (approx.) · capital Altava</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A post-Roman Berber kingdom in western Algeria whose king Masuna called himself ruler of both Moors and Romans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As Vandal control of the interior weakened in the late 5th century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> leaders in the former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mauretania-caesariensis">Mauretania Caesariensis</a> set up their own states. An inscription at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altava">Altava</a> (Ouled Mimoun) dated 508 names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masuna">Masuna</a> as 'king of the peoples of the Moors and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>', the best evidence for this kingdom, which probably arose in the 470s or 480s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribal leadership with surviving Roman towns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscriptions and Christian communities, and kept Roman provincial dating. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> records that a Moorish ruler named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masuna">Massonas</a>, probably the same Masuna, allied with the Byzantine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> against other Berber rulers in 535. Its exact borders are unknown, but it was centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> region and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> plain.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garmul">Garmul</a>, a later king, attacked <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/praetorian-prefecture-of-africa">Byzantine Africa</a> in the 570s and was killed by the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gennadius-exarch-of-africa">Gennadius</a> in 578. Power in the region then passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-altava">Kingdom of Altava</a>, which survived until the Arab conquest.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-the-moors-and-romans.jpg" alt="Kingdom of the Moors and Romans" width="320" height="170"><figcaption>Image: Ichthyovenator · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mauro-Roman_Kingdom.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>477 CE – 578 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Altava</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Masuna, Garmul</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mauro-Roman Kingdom, Kingdom of Masuna, Kingdom of Altava (Masuna)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=528&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-moors-and-romans" aria-label="Kingdom of the Moors and Romans on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4654 915 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of the Moors and Romans lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4974 1171L5062 1084M4974 1171L4974 1171"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5062 1084h0M4974 1171h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4974 1171h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4974 1171h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Altava. <a href="/atlas?year=528&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-moors-and-romans">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
